issue.body:
### Affected versions

Reproduced on the current 4.10.x branch (`16ab9a543c`) and the current 5.2.x branch (`5f25fa9aa9`), using the reproducer below. Reading the code, it is long-standing: a start has always processed the property sources straight into the catalogs that lookups read. It is not a regression of a recent release.

### Summary

`Environment#start()` processes the property sources one after another into the published property catalogs, writing their keys into the catalogs' `LinkedHashMap`s. Lookups read those maps without the lock of the catalogs, and some of them iterate the maps. #14140 lists these lookups: `getProperty(name, Map.class)`, `getProperties(name)`, `containsProperties(name)`, `getAllProperties(...)` and others.

Another thread can run such a lookup while the start writes a key with the same first letter. The lookup then fails with `ConcurrentModificationException`, or it sees only part of the start's properties. The same happens when an environment is stopped and started again.

This is the start's side of #14140. The fix for #14140 (#14167 on 5.2.x) covers an add on a running environment. A refresh already builds its catalogs off to the side and publishes them in one step: since 4.10.32 (#13896) and since 5.2.0 (#13079). A start, including a start after `stop()`, still writes in place.

### Reproducer

`DefaultEnvironment` is package-private on 5.2.x, so this runs in package `io.micronaut.context.env`, in a test for example. `ParkedValue` is the `CharSequence` from the #14140 reproducer, whose `toString()` parks once armed.

```java
// a property source, processed after "data", whose iterator() parks once armed
final class GatedSource implements PropertySource {
    final AtomicBoolean armed = new AtomicBoolean();
    final CountDownLatch entered = new CountDownLatch(1);
    final CountDownLatch release = new CountDownLatch(1);

    @Override public String getName() { return "gated"; }
    @Override public int getOrder() { return 2; }
    @Override public Object get(String key) { return "app.third".equals(key) ? "three" : null; }

    @Override
    public Iterator<String> iterator() {
        if (armed.compareAndSet(true, false)) {
            entered.countDown();
            try {
                release.await(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        }
        return List.of("app.third").iterator();
    }
}

ParkedValue parked = new ParkedValue();
GatedSource gated = new GatedSource();
DefaultEnvironment env = new DefaultEnvironment((ApplicationContextConfiguration) ApplicationContext.builder()
        .deduceEnvironment(false)
        .enableDefaultPropertySources(false));
env.addPropertySource(PropertySource.of("data", Map.of("app.first", parked, "app.second", "two"), 1));
env.addPropertySource(gated);
// for a restart, first call env.start() and env.stop() here

gated.armed.set(true);
Thread starter = new Thread(env::start);
starter.start();
gated.entered.await();                               // the start has processed "data" and parks in "gated"

parked.armed.set(true);
AtomicReference<Throwable> failure = new AtomicReference<>();
Thread lookup = new Thread(() -> {
    try {
        env.getProperty("app", Map.class);
    } catch (Throwable t) {
        failure.set(t);
    }
});
lookup.start();
parked.reached.await();                              // the lookup is iterating the map of "a" keys
gated.release.countDown();
starter.join();                                      // the start has written app.third into that map
parked.release.countDown();
lookup.join();
System.out.println(failure.get());                   // java.util.ConcurrentModificationException
```

| Branch | During a start | During a start after `stop()` |
|---|---|---|
| 4.10.x (`16ab9a543c`) | `ConcurrentModificationException` | `ConcurrentModificationException` |
| 5.2.x (`5f25fa9aa9`) | `ConcurrentModificationException` | `ConcurrentModificationException` |

### Impact

The impact is lower than for #14140, because a lookup has to run on another thread while the environment starts. In a typical application the environment starts in `ApplicationContext#start()`, before the beans that look up properties on other threads exist. So the issue mostly concerns code that stops and starts an environment while other threads use it.

### Possible fix

Build the start's catalogs off to the side and publish them in one step, as a refresh does.

- **4.10.x:** a start already holds the lock of the catalogs for its whole read (since 4.10.32), so it could reuse the refresh's mechanism. Two behaviours need deciding:
  - what lookups on other threads see during the start;
  - what a start that fails leaves behind. Today it leaves the properties it processed before it failed.
- **5.2.x:** a start does not hold that lock across its read. Building off to the side therefore also has to settle what a refresh during the start does, which is #14139. One way is for the start to hold the lock for its whole read, as on 4.10.x. A refresh or an add from another thread would then wait for the whole start, the kind of wait in #14141, #14158 and #14160.

