issue.body:
Part of the development-mode reloading plan, micronaut-projects/micronaut-core#13654.

## Problem

Seven module PRs that adopt development-mode reloading need the same answer from a class change applied in place: which bean definitions are built from the changed classes. Those PRs are micronaut-projects/micronaut-gcp#1553, micronaut-projects/micronaut-jms#791, micronaut-projects/micronaut-kafka#1413, micronaut-projects/micronaut-mqtt#664, micronaut-projects/micronaut-nats#716, micronaut-projects/micronaut-rabbitmq#887 and micronaut-projects/micronaut-cache#1078, and micronaut-projects/micronaut-validation#705 needs it too. Each module uses the answer to find its listener, serializer, client, cached or validated beans.

The event gives only class names, so each module guessed the definitions from the names of generated classes. It took `$Name$Definition` and `$Name$Definition$...` for the class and its proxies, `$Name$Intercepted$Definition` for an introduced interface, and `$Factory$...$Definition` with a declaring-type check for factory products. That copies the compiler's naming scheme into eight modules. It also misses beans whose superclass or interface changed.

A change applied in place retires no classloader, so `isStaleDefinition` does not help. The definitions of the context stay as they were, and the changed classes run new code.

## Change

`ClassChangeEvent#affectsDefinition(BeanDefinition)` (experimental, as the whole event is) says whether a definition is built from a class in the change set. That holds when the change set contains any of these:

- the generated definition class itself;
- the bean type, or one of its supertypes: a superclass, or an interface it implements, directly or through another interface, whose default methods it runs;
- the factory that declares a factory method or field definition (or a superclass of it);
- the class an `AdvisedBeanType` intercepts: the interface of introduction advice, or the class of around advice;
- for a delegating definition (`@EachBean`, `@EachProperty` entries), whatever its target is built from;
- for a proxy definition, its target definition class or target type.

The checks go by class name, so the answer also holds for a change that retires a loader. The rules follow `isStaleDefinition`, which answers the same question by classloader.

A module now selects the definitions it cares about from the context, for example with `getBeanDefinitions(type, qualifier)` or `getAllBeanDefinitions()`. It keeps those for which the event says `affectsDefinition`. No generated name is matched.

`affects(String)` now looks names up in a set built once with the event, instead of scanning the list.

Nothing outside development mode calls the method. No production path changes.

## Tests

`ClassChangeEventDefinitionSpec` (inject-java) compiles beans and checks:

- a bean type, a superclass of it, and an interface it implements directly or through another interface, are affected. A class that only shares the name prefix (`Plain` and `PlainExtra`) is not;
- a nested class counts as a class of its own: changing `Outer` does not affect `Outer$Inner`;
- a factory product is affected by a change of the factory or of the product;
- around advice with `proxyTarget = true`: both the proxy definition and the target definition are affected by a change of the advised class;
- introduction advice is affected by a change of the introduced interface. This case needs the `AdvisedBeanType` branch; the test fails without it;
- `@EachProperty` delegating definitions are affected through their target;
- the generated definition class itself counts;
- an empty change affects nothing, and a null definition is never affected.

The guide's development-mode watch section has a paragraph and an example on the new method.

## Modules

The module PRs listed above replace their name matching with this method. They build only once this is merged and a core 5.3.0 snapshot is published.

